      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIM",
          "content": "The aim of this study was to assess postoperative analgesia after preincisional and postincisional administration of remifentanil."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Randomized trial, 24 hours."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "University hospital, hospitalized care."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS",
          "content": "48 adult patients scheduled for lumbar vertebral surgery."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTIONS",
          "content": "in group R5, patients received an infusion of 0.2 microg kg(-1) min(-1) remifentanil over 5 minutes, followed by a break of 15 minutes before anesthesia was started. Anesthesia was induced by infusion of 0.25 microg kg(-1) min(-1) remifentanil and a bolus of 1.5 microg kg(-1) propofol, followed by a continuous infusion of 2 to 3 microg kg(-1) h-1 propofol and 0.25 microg kg(-1) min(-1) remifentanil until end of anesthesia. In group R20, patients received 0.05 microg kg(-1) min(-1) remifentanil over 20 minutes before the induction of anesthesia. In group RL, anesthesia was induced and maintained with propofol. After surgery began, a remifentanil infusion of 0.5 microg kg(-1) min(-1) was given for 50 minutes, then reduced to 0.25 microg kg(-1) min(-1). The total remifentanil doses were similar in the 3 groups."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MEASURES",
          "content": "patients used patient-controlled analgesia (piritramide) for postoperative pain management. They recorded pain on a numeric rating scale every half hour."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "STATISTICS",
          "content": "Kruskal-Wallis test, pairwise Mann-Withney U-test, orthogonal polynomials (pain scores)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "PATIENTS given postincisional remifentanil (RL) had the slowest decrease in postoperative pain scores (p<0.01) and the highest cumulative piritramide consumption (p<0.08)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "The preincisional administration of remifentanil followed by a continuous infusion of 0.25 microg kg(-1) min(-1) appears to reduce pain scores and piritramid consumption when compared with a postincisional regimen."
        }
      ]},
